  "status": "completed",
  "output": "Delta simulation complete and verified ✅\n\n**1. Simulated the source change**\n- Appended `4,Dan,dan@example.com,Ogdenville` to `customers.csv` (now 4 data rows)\n- Note: the snapshot `customers_snapshot.csv` still has the original 3 rows — it's holding the pre-delta baseline as intended\n\n**2. Captured the delta** (`delta_sync.py`)\n- Logic: read all CSV rows, query existing ids from the `customers` table, keep only ids not yet in the DB\n- Result: `Captured delta: 1 row(s): [(4, 'Dan')]`\n\n**3. Applied the delta**\n- Inserted Dan's row and committed → `Applied 1 row(s)`, table now has 4 rows\n\n**Verification** (`verify.py`): CSV ↔ DB row-for-row match is `True`:\n\n| id | name | email | city |\n|----|------|-------|------|\n| 1 | Alice | alice@example.com | Springfield |\n| 2 | Bob | bob@example.com | Shelbyville |\n| 3 | Carol | carol@example.com | Capital City |\n| 4 | Dan | dan@example.com | Ogdenville |\n\nThe delta script is idempotent by design — running it again with no new rows would capture 0 rows and change nothing. Current workspace assets: `customers.csv` (live source), `customers_snapshot.csv` (baseline), `customers.db` (target), plus `load_customers.py` (backfill), `delta_sync.py` (incremental sync), and `verify.py`.",
